From b229578e349483c36c08e677baf9dcb83408fef2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mykola Lysenko <mykolal@meta.com>
Date: Sat, 25 Jul 2026 20:23:46 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] um: x86: add DYNAMIC_FTRACE with direct calls for BPF
 trampolines

Give 64-bit UML a working ftrace stack so fentry/fexit/fmod_ret/LSM
BPF programs can attach:

- Switch from -mcmodel=large to small: the image links below 2GB, and
  the large model made -mfentry emit 12-byte movabs+indirect-call
  sequences that no x86 patching machinery (ftrace, X86_PATCH_SIZE,
  bpf_arch_text_poke) can handle. Small model restores the canonical
  5-byte fentry call.
- Carve a 256MB module/execmem window immediately below the kernel
  image (the mirror of native x86-64's top-2GB module area): keeps
  small-model module R_X86_64_32S relocations representable and every
  patched rel32 call site (ftrace direct calls into BPF trampolines,
  kfunc calls) in range. Extend the kernel-mode fault window in
  segv() so lazily-synced kernel mappings there resolve.
- Select HAVE_(FUNCTION_TRACER|DYNAMIC_FTRACE|WITH_ARGS|
  WITH_DIRECT_CALLS|FTRACE_MCOUNT_RECORD|FENTRY); mcount tables come
  from scripts/recordmcount.
- arch/um/kernel/ftrace.c: 5-byte call/nop patching via memcpy inside
  an os_protect_memory(RW->RX) window; the UML kernel is a host
  process and may patch its own text directly.
- arch/x86/um/ftrace_64.S: WITH_ARGS ftrace_caller building a partial
  um pt_regs (HOST_* gp slots) frame, function_trace_op dispatch, and
  the shared-site direct-call tail jump via the ORIG_AX stash.
- Drop the patchable_function_entry(5,0) hack from the __x64_sys_
  wrappers: with real fentry instrumentation it produced pre-nopped
  entries that tripped ftrace_bug() at boot.
- Strip CC_FLAGS_FTRACE from USER_CFLAGS: host-side objects must not
  call the kernel's __fentry__.

Validated in-guest: modify_return, tracing_struct (module fentry with
struct args), get_func_args_test, recursion, trampoline_count,
fexit_stress all pass; boot nops 26,648 recorded sites cleanly.

Signed-off-by: Mykola Lysenko <mykolal@meta.com>
